MID_SE_Dan_Ned_C: <EDH>
MID_SE_Dan_Ned_C_001: Dante, I read your latest play. I'm curious to know\nwhat you're working on next.
MID_SE_Dan_Ned_C_002: Ah, piqued your interest, did it? Please, tell me if\nthere were any parts you particularly enjoyed.
MID_SE_Dan_Ned_C_003: You're from Saramis, right? Therefore, stab in\nthe dark, your favorite part must be— Hang on.
MID_SE_Dan_Ned_C_004: You're from Saramis!
MID_SE_Dan_Ned_C_005: Your kingdom expressly forbids the written word.
MID_SE_Dan_Ned_C_006: And yet you casually mention that you have drunk\nthe inky elixir of the glyph!
MID_SE_Dan_Ned_C_007: I learned the written word after I left home. It was\nessential for becoming a mercenary.
MID_SE_Dan_Ned_C_008: How else could I read requests for help? Or do\nbusiness with customers who want contracts?
MID_SE_Dan_Ned_C_009: Taking up reading was, in essence, another skill I\ncould put in service of battling.
MID_SE_Dan_Ned_C_010: I'll assimilate everything I must to become the\nstrongest warrior I can be.
MID_SE_Dan_Ned_C_011: You devour life with an omnivorous hunger. Yes!\nAs a master auteur who prizes research, I can relate.
MID_SE_Dan_Ned_C_012: Is that so? Well, I've gotten a fair share of tactics\nfrom your plays.
MID_SE_Dan_Ned_C_013: In my depictions of character, you mean? Their\nfeelings, their interiority?
MID_SE_Dan_Ned_C_014: In the way you portray battles. In particular, your\nepic stage fight in Tragic King Croesus.
MID_SE_Dan_Ned_C_015: That style of fierce swordplay applies to a range of\nbody movement when you're facing multiple foes.
MID_SE_Dan_Ned_C_016: I've learned that a well-crafted story can serve\nas good fodder for battle experience.
MID_SE_Dan_Ned_C_017: Indeed—reality itself blanches at my plays' level\nof realism.
MID_SE_Dan_Ned_C_018: Still, I'm honored you let my words influence your\nfighting style. No wonder it looked so familiar.
MID_SE_Dan_Ned_C_019: I'll observe you more closely in battle next\ntime around.
MID_SE_Dan_Ned_C_020: Who knows? You may even inspire my next work!
MID_SE_Dan_Ned_C_021: That would be fine by me. I'd be glad to serve as\nyour muse.
MID_SE_Dan_Ned_B: <EDH>
MID_SE_Dan_Ned_B_001: Haaah!
MID_SE_Dan_Ned_B_002: Hyah!
MID_SE_Dan_Ned_B_003: Excellent. We've routed the enemy. Mission\naccomplished.
MID_SE_Dan_Ned_B_004: Marvelous! Why, the fluidity of your movements\nreminded me of the genuine article.
MID_SE_Dan_Ned_B_005: That is, the climactic battle scene as I depicted it in\nTragic King Croesus!
MID_SE_Dan_Ned_B_006: I'm honored that you noticed. Say, I'd like to ask\nyou something I've wondered for a while...
MID_SE_Dan_Ned_B_007: The magical talents you displayed back there...\nWhere did you learn them?
MID_SE_Dan_Ned_B_008: Through my literary pursuits, of course!
MID_SE_Dan_Ned_B_009: I was writing a play about a mage and picked up the\nfundamentals from interviewing him.
MID_SE_Dan_Ned_B_010: You call your level of ability..."the fundamentals"?
MID_SE_Dan_Ned_B_011: I've learned to handle all sorts of weapons in my\nresearch for projects.
MID_SE_Dan_Ned_B_012: From instructors at the temples I learned sword\ntechniques, bow techniques.
MID_SE_Dan_Ned_B_013: Then, as the pursuit of authenticity so often requires,\nI added flourishes from my own imagination.
MID_SE_Dan_Ned_B_014: I see. So your mastery and mine are both drawing\nfrom each other's fields. Intriguing.
MID_SE_Dan_Ned_B_015: Still, you're a playwright and a skilled warrior. I am\nin awe of your talent.
MID_SE_Dan_Ned_B_016: Oh, you flatter me! You know, I have an idea\nfor my next work. Tell me how this sounds.
MID_SE_Dan_Ned_B_017: It stars a warrior who can cut down legions in battle,\nbut deep down, aspires to be a playwright.
MID_SE_Dan_Ned_B_018: Thus, she is thrust into the hardest skirmish of all—\nmastering the written word.
MID_SE_Dan_Ned_B_019: Your new play sounds like it's about you.
MID_SE_Dan_Ned_B_020: I'm not a warrior, I'm a playwright who dabbles.
MID_SE_Dan_Ned_B_021: No, the protagonist I speak of is not me—it is us!\nOur two lives combined into one.
MID_SE_Dan_Ned_B_022: After all, we are similar in our voracious appetite for\nexperience.
MID_SE_Dan_Ned_B_023: It's about me as well? Even more intriguing.
MID_SE_Dan_Ned_B_024: I know, it's genius. Shall we begin the interview?\nA talk with Nydine, the literate legend of Saramis!
MID_SE_Dan_Ned_B_025: I'll reward your participation by teaching you some\nof the fighting techniques I've honed.
MID_SE_Dan_Ned_B_026: I will look forward to that. What better teacher than\none of the great talents of our time!